\section{Giving MASE and RMSSE their scale}{

The scaled errors (\code{mase}, \code{rmsse}) divide each row's error by its series'
in-sample naive-forecast error, so they need each series' history. There are
two ways to give it, and they give identical scores wherever both apply:
\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{history = train}}, the common case: the data the model was trained
on. The prediction already knows its time, series and response columns
(it records them), so nothing else is needed. \code{period} sets the naive
forecast's lag: 1, or the season's length for the seasonal naive.
\item \strong{\code{naive = ilm_naive_scale(train, time =, group =, response =)}}: the
scale computed once and passed in. Use it when the prediction doesn't know
its time column (see \emph{Time and series columns} in \code{\link[=ilm_predict]{ilm_predict()}}), or when several
models' predictions must share one scale, so their scaled errors compare
on the same footing.
}

When \verb{history =} can't work, because the prediction has no time column, the
error says so and gives the \verb{naive =} call to use instead. Give one of the
two, not both.
}

